# coinmarketcap.rb/CHANGELOG

## 20260915

0.6.4: Rewrite the CHANGELOG in the standard form, and the README's fork URL, which named another project.

1. ~ CHANGELOG.txt --> CHANGELOG, as every other gem here spells it; + the title, + ## before each date, + a blank line between each summary and its first item. No item is reworded, dropped or reordered.
2. ~ test/VERSION_test.rb: /CHANGELOG.txt/CHANGELOG/. It reads the file by name, so the rename alone left it erroring upon a path which no longer exists.
3. ~ CHANGELOG: /202503030/20250330/, nine digits where eight belong. 0.4.1 was committed 20250330, and the heading sits between 20250307 and 20250513, which agrees.
4. ~ README.md: the fork URL named another repository, having been copied from it, so it sent anyone following it to the wrong project.
5. ~ CoinMarketCap::VERSION: /0.6.3/0.6.4/


## 20260910

0.6.3: ~ lib/CoinMarketCap.rb: + require_relative './CoinMarketCap/VERSION'; + test/VERSION_test.rb

1. ~ lib/CoinMarketCap.rb: + `require_relative './CoinMarketCap/VERSION'`, last among the requires. The file has been present and unreached, so `require 'coinmarketcap.rb'` left CoinMarketCap::VERSION undefined and the gemspec was the only thing loading it.
2. + test/VERSION_test.rb: that CoinMarketCap::VERSION is a string, that it is three numbers separated by dots, and that it matches the newest entry in the changelog.
3. The test loads the library rather than VERSION.rb, which is the whole of its value: run against v0.6.2 it fails with "uninitialized constant CoinMarketCap::VERSION". Nine libraries were in that state and not one of them carried this test, which is how it went unseen.
4. ~ CoinMarketCap::VERSION: /0.6.2/0.6.3/


